      "note": "Nourai's root box is an accurate copy of Pokorny p.581: language label (Indo-European), the three ablaut shapes Kerk / Kork / Krk and the gloss 'thin, slender' all match Pokorny's '[kerk-], kork- : kr̥k-, Meaning: to wrinkle, become thin', whose material is exactly Old Indic kr̥śa- / kr̥śyati, Avestan kərəsa- 'skinny', Latin gracilis (dissim. from *cracilis) and Lith. karšti 'to grow old'. Pokorny himself adds a caution ('Note: or at most kark- : kr̥k-'); Wiktionary and modern handbooks write the root with a palatal, *kerḱ-, since Skt. kr̥śá- and Av. kərəsa- require *ḱ. The AHD/Watkins appendix does not carry this root at all, so Nourai could not have taken it from there. No node of this entry is in conflict with modern scholarship; only the direction of the Avestan→Sogdian arrow is a simplification (see node 3).",
