          "derivation": "Young Avestan kərəsa- is a real adjective 'mager, lean' (Bartholomae, Altiranisches Wörterbuch, col. 469-470: 'j. ¹kərəsa- Adj. \"mager\". Komp[ositionsglied]. — ai. kr̥śá- Adj.'), the exact cognate of Vedic kr̥śá- and the regular Iranian reflex of PIE *kr̥ḱ-so- (PIE *ḱ > Proto-Iranian *s, and the syllabic *r̥ appears in Avestan as the anaptyctic ərə). Both compounds in the box are bahuvrīhis attested as personal names: kərəsa-vazdah- (AiW col. 470, 'j. kərəsa-vazdah- m. EN. eines Bruders' — the brother of Fraŋrasyan/Afrāsiāb) = kərəsa- 'little' + vazdah- 'firmness, endurance' (AiW col. 1379 'g. ¹vazdah- Adj. \"beständig\"'), so literally 'whose endurance is scant' — Nourai's gloss 'with little power' is right, though he drops the final -h of the stem; and kərəsāspa- = kərəsa- + aspa- 'horse' with the regular contraction a+a > ā, 'he whose horses are lean' (Encyclopaedia Iranica, KARSĀSP: 'Av. Kərəsāspa probably means \"he with meager horses\"; cf. Old Indic kr̥śvāśva', citing Mayrhofer 1977 I/60). Nourai's uncontracted spelling 'keresa-aspa' is a morphological analysis, not the attested form Kərəsāspa-.",
