          "derivation": "Both New Persian names continue the Avestan compounds of node 1 through Middle Persian. Av. Kərəsavazdah- → MP Karsēwaz/Garsēwaz → NP گرسیوز Garsivaz, the Turanian, brother of Afrāsiāb in the Šāh-nāma: the stem-final -dah is lost as regularly in MP (cf. Av. vazdah- 'firmness'), and Av. -a- of the second member appears as Persian -ē-/-i-. Av. Kərəsāspa- → MP Kirsāsp/Karsāsp (attested in the Pahlavi books, cf. Iranica: 'Av. Kərəsāspa, Pahlavi various forms, Pers. Garšāsp (Garšāsb, Arabicized Karšāsf)') → NP گرشاسپ Garšāsp, the dragon-slaying hero of Asadi Ṭusi's Garšāsp-nāma. The one irregularity is the initial voicing k- > g-: Skjærvø in Iranica calls the change 'problematic' and conjectures contamination with Gar-šāh 'king of the mountain(s)', the epithet of Gayōmard, while Mo'in and Dehkhoda argue that old manuscripts did not distinguish kāf from gāf in writing, so that Kar-/Gar- is largely an orthographic matter (Abu'l-Fidāʾ still writes کرشاسف). The glosses Nourai gives ('with little power', 'with lean horses') are precisely Mo'in's.",
