        {
          "id": 4,
          "lang": "Latin",
          "words": "gracilis",
          "verdict": "confirmed",
          "derivation": "Latin gracilis, -e 'thin, lean, slender' (opposed to pinguis; Ernout–Meillet s.v., p.497) is put under this root by Pokorny 581 and Klein 672. The formation is an -ilis adjective of the habilis type, built on a lost verb *graceō/*craceō 'to be meagre', whose participle survives in the Festus gloss 'cracentes (for gra-), graciles' (Paul. Fest. 46,16); Pokorny takes gracilis to be dissimilated from *cracilis, i.e. the initial g- is secondary (Solmsen KZ 34, 21 explains gr-c- < cr-c- by dissimilation). The root vocalism *kr̥ḱ- gives Latin -ra- as usual for syllabic *r̥. Wiktionary states the chain directly: 'From an Old Latin verb *graceō or *craceō \"to be meager, slim\", from Proto-Indo-European *kerḱ- \"to become thin, to wane\", related to Sanskrit कृश (kṛśa), Lithuanian karštu, and Avestan 𐬐𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬯𐬀 (kərəsa)'. Ernout–Meillet are more reserved: they set up only *graceō and do not mention the Indo-Iranian cognates, so the Indo-Iranian connection, while standard, is not universally asserted.",
